<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Manco Capac</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">fl. c. 1200</span> · <a href="/centuries/13th-century">13th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Manco Capac is the legendary founder of the Inca dynasty and of the city of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cusco">Cusco</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/inca-empire">Inca</a> tradition says he emerged with his siblings from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lake-titicaca">Lake Titicaca</a> or from the cave of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pacaritambo">Pacaritambo</a> and led his people to the Cusco valley, where his golden staff sank into the earth. He stands at the head of the royal line that later built the Inca Empire.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/manco-capac.jpg" alt="Manco Capac" width="320" height="343" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Brooklyn_Museum_-_Manco_Capac,_First_Inca,_1_of_14_Portraits_of_Inca_Kings.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Manco Cápac, also known as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manco-inca">Manco Inca</a> and Ayar Manco, was according to some historians the first governor and founder of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/inca-empire">Inca</a> civilisation in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cusco">Cusco</a>, possibly in the early 13th century. Although several chronicles mention him, his actual existence remains uncertain. In the account given as history, he was born in Tamputoco, which some locate in the present-day province of Paruro in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peru">Peru</a>, the son of Apu Tambo, head of a local tribal <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ayllu">ayllu</a> of several dozen families. The family lived a nomadic life, and on his father's death Manco Cápac succeeded him as head of the ayllu. His main wife, his sister Mama Uqllu, bore his son and successor, Sinchi Ruq'a.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>On reaching the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cusco">Cusco</a> valley the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ayllu">ayllu</a> defeated three small tribes, the Sahuares, Huallas and Alcahuisas, and settled in a swampy area between two small streams, where the main plaza of Cusco stands today. The new city was divided into four districts: Chumbicancha, Quinticancha, Sairecancha and Yarambuycancha. His ayllu occupied only a small part of the valley, and larger tribes, among them a confederated lordship of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ayarmaca">Ayarmacas</a> and Pinaguas to the north, regarded the newcomers as invaders and often attacked them. Manco Cápac and later his son had to defend the city against them. He died a natural death and left Cusco to his son <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sinchi-roca">Sinchi Roca</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>His body was mummified and remained in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cusco">Cusco</a> until <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pachacuti">Pachacuti</a> ordered it moved to the Temple of the Sun on Isla del Sol, leaving a statue in the city. He is the protagonist of the two best-known legends of the origin of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/inca-empire">Inca</a>. In the first, he is Ayar Manco, a son of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/viracocha">Viracocha</a> of Paqariq Tampu, who with his brothers and sisters set out to conquer the tribes of the Cusco valley, and in some versions got rid of his brothers by trapping them or turning them to stone.</p><p>In the second, he is a son of the sun god <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/inti">Inti</a> and the moon goddess Mama Killa, who emerged with his siblings from the cave of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pacaritambo">Pacaritambo</a> carrying the golden staff tapac-yauri and was told to build a Temple of the Sun where it sank into the earth. No written tradition of this second tale is recorded before Garcilaso de la Vega's Comentarios Reales de los Incas of 1609.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Manco Cápac” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Their origin myths tell of <b>Manco Capac</b> and his siblings emerging from the cave of Pacaritambo, or from Lake Titicaca, to found Cusco.</blockquote><a href="/empire/inca-empire">Inca Empire · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>Garcilaso de la Vega, who calls them “Incas by privilege”, traces the grant back to <b>Manco Capac</b>, the dynasty’s legendary founder, and says they were allowed to wear the headband and to pierce their ears like the Incas of the blood, though with marks of a lower degree.</blockquote><a href="/societies/inca-empire">Social Hierarchy of the Inca Empire</a></li><li><blockquote>Inca tradition says the founder <b>Manco Capac</b> emerged from Lake Titicaca or the cave of Pacaritambo and led his people to the valley of Cusco, founding the city around 1200.</blockquote><a href="/state/kingdom-of-cusco">Kingdom of Cusco</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Paruro Province, in what is now <a href="/countries/peru">Peru</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cusco">Cusco</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/peru">Peru</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=-13.76,-71.85&amp;year=1200" aria-label="Paruro Province on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="2582 2676 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Manco Capac"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M3032 2946h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M3032 2946h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=-13.76,-71.85&amp;year=1200">What was here in 1200? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
